A||||2||Hartz||HELP!  Backlit cluster swap||||5||
Z||000000||Hartz||06-24-2001||11:56 AM||||I picked up a backlit cluster out of what looked to be an 86-87 SE yesterday at the boneyard.  It's only 85 MPH, but it's still an improvement over my old '85 cluster.<P>Question - when I plugged it in, everything appeared to work but the tach reading was wrong.  It read maybe 2800 RPM too high.  Coincidently (?) the needed idled in approximately the same location as the old tach.  Is the tach broke, did the needle get jarred, or is it because zero RPM on the old cluster was approximately 8 o'clock, and the new tach is maybe at 5 o'clock?<P>If it isn't broke, what can I do to get it to read correctly?<P>Any help appreciated.<P>Hartz<BR>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroJoe||06-24-2001||10:42 PM||||Hmm, well, if it was out of an 87, and it was the 85mph speedo, then it must have been a 4 cyl.  87 4's get tach signals in a very different way than earlier 4's with HEI ignition.  I believe the ECM gets a tach signal from the DIS coils or something like that.  If you can be sure that you got it out of an 86, then you dont have to worry about any of this.<P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000002||fieroluke||06-25-2001||03:19 AM||||Hi Hartz,<P>there's and article on my Fiero pages (www.fieros.de) that describes how to recalibrate your tach to your wishes (not just by swapping a capacitor, which can have tolerances of up to 20%)<P>Best regards,<P>-Oliver<P>------------------<BR>Visit my website: <A HREF="http://www.fieros.de" TARGET=_blank>www.fieros.de</A>  for lots of technical and other Fiero related information<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fieros.de/jpg/mycars.jpg"> ||||reg||1
